{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>His sons and the sons of, etc.</b> Since it said in the preceding verse ״Jacob and all his seed,״ there was no need to say a second time ״his sons and the sons of״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> one needs to know why it separated between ״his sons and his sons' sons״ and ״his daughters and his sons' daughters and all his seed״ with the word אתו, and was it not sufficient with what it said at the end, ״he brought with him to Egypt״?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the verse's intention is to inform us that there is a difference among the sons of Jacob in their descent to Egypt, for some of them came of their own will to accept the decree of the King with a whole heart, and some of them were hesitant to hold back from descending into the iron furnace, and the verse indicated who and who were those who went to discharge the bond-debt of exile of their own will. And it says <b>his sons and his sons' sons with him</b> — meaning, these did not need to be brought; rather, they themselves came with him, like him. And afterward it arranged those who did not come of their own will, until Jacob needed to bring them down against their will — and that is what it says <b>his daughters and his sons' daughters and all his seed</b>, meaning the sons of his sons' sons — these ״he brought with him to Egypt,״ meaning, he brought them; they did not come of their own will." "<b>And I have seen fit</b> to give attention to the words of the Sages, their memory for blessing (Shemot Rabbah 1:5), who said that as long as one of those who went down to Egypt was alive, the servitude did not begin, as it is written (Exodus 1:6): ״And Joseph died… and all that generation״ — meaning, once they passed away the servitude began. For perhaps because they accepted the decree of the King willingly, the servitude was lifted from them, for the suffering of acceptance [itself] may be considered its remedy. And a faithful witness to this as well: Jochebed and Serah the daughter of Asher were among those who went down to Egypt (Genesis Rabbah 94:9), and in their days there was servitude, and the reason is that Jacob brought them and not of their own will. And see the words of the Zohar (Vayakhel 198:) in explanation of the verse ״Happy is he who has the El of Jacob for his help, whose hope is in YHWH״ (Psalms 146:5)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:09.532428181Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}